window.MATHEMATICS_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"number-bases","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Number bases: converting and calculating","body":"JAMB tests converting between bases and doing simple arithmetic in a base.\n\nConverting TO base ten: multiply each digit by the base raised to its place value, then add.\n• 1011_{2} = 1×2^{3} + 0×2^{2} + 1×2^{1} + 1×2^{0} = 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11.\n• 214_{5} = 2×25 + 1×5 + 4 = 59.\n\nConverting FROM base ten: divide repeatedly by the new base and read the remainders from the bottom up.\n• 59 in base 5: 59 ÷ 5 = 11 r 4, 11 ÷ 5 = 2 r 1, 2 ÷ 5 = 0 r 2 → 214_{5}.\n\nBetween two bases that are not ten, go through base ten.\n\nFinding an unknown base: if 25_{x} = 17_{10}, then 2x + 5 = 17, so x = 6.\n\nArithmetic in a base: work as usual, but \"carry\" when you reach the base. In base 5, 4 + 3 = 7 = 1×5 + 2, so write 2, carry 1.\n\nCommon traps:\n• A digit can never equal or exceed the base (no 5 in base 5). Use this to rule out options fast.\n• The right-hand digit has place value base^{0} = 1, not the base.\n\nMemory tip: \"multiply and add\" to get into base ten; \"divide and read remainders upwards\" to get out."},"new":{"title":"Number bases: converting and calculating","body":"INTRODUCTION — WHY NUMBER BASES MATTER\nA number base tells us how many different digits are used before we carry into the next column. Ordinary counting uses base ten (digits 0–9). Binary uses 0 and 1. Computers process binary instructions; other bases are convenient ways of representing binary values.\n\n1. PLACE VALUE AND VALID DIGITS\nIn base b, allowed digits are 0, 1, …, b−1. The columns, from right to left, have values b⁰, b¹, b², b³ and so on. A digit equal to or larger than b is invalid. Therefore 245₅ is impossible because digit 5 is not allowed in base 5.\nFor fractional parts, place values to the right of the point are b⁻¹, b⁻², etc.\n\n2. CONVERSION TO BASE TEN\nMultiply each digit by its place value, then add.\n1011₂ = 1×8 + 0×4 + 1×2 + 1×1 = 11₁₀.\n243₅ = 2×25 + 4×5 + 3 = 73₁₀.\n10.11₂ = 1×2 + 0×1 + 1×½ + 1×¼ = 2.75₁₀.\nA decimal fraction in a base does NOT automatically mean an ordinary base-ten decimal fraction.\n\n3. CONVERSION FROM BASE TEN\nFor a whole number, divide repeatedly by the new base. Write down the remainders and read them upwards.\n45₁₀ ÷ 2: 45=2×22+1; 22=2×11+0; 11=2×5+1; 5=2×2+1; 2=2×1+0; 1=2×0+1. Thus 45₁₀=101101₂.\nFor a fractional part, repeatedly MULTIPLY by the base, recording each whole-number part in order: 0.625×2=1.25 (digit 1), 0.25×2=0.5 (digit 0), 0.5×2=1.0 (digit 1). Hence 0.625₁₀=0.101₂.\nFor a base-to-base conversion, a safe route is source base → base ten → destination base.\n\n4. ADDITION, SUBTRACTION AND MULTIPLICATION\nIn base 5, 34₅+23₅: units 4+3=7=12₅, write 2 and carry 1; fives 3+2+1=6=11₅, write 1 carry 1. Answer: 112₅. Verify in base ten: 19+13=32; 112₅=25+5+2=32.\nSubtraction uses borrowing ONE unit from the next column, worth b units in the current column.\nFor multiplication, you may convert both numbers to base ten and convert the product back if this makes checking easier.\n\n5. UNKNOWN BASES\nIf 132ₓ=42₁₀, then x²+3x+2=42, so x²+3x−40=0=(x+8)(x−5). Since a base must exceed the largest digit (3), x=5, not −8.\n\n6. MODULAR ARITHMETIC\na≡r (mod m) means a and r give the same remainder when divided by m. Example: 17≡2 (mod 5).\nClock arithmetic uses modulo 12: four hours after 10 o'clock is 2 o'clock.\nTo compute 19+27 (mod 7), 19≡5 and 27≡6, so 5+6=11≡4 (mod 7).\n\nVISUAL CHECK — COLUMN VALUES\nThe place-value diagram below shows why 1011₂ equals eleven in base ten.\n\nJAMB-STYLE WORKED EXAMPLE\nConvert 101.101₂ to base ten: 1×4+0×2+1×1+1×½+0×¼+1×⅛ = 5.625₁₀.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Reading base digits as ordinary decimal digits.\n• Putting the least significant bit on the left.\n• Reading division remainders from top to bottom.\n• Forgetting the fractional base powers are negative.\n• Accepting a negative or too-small solution for an unknown base.\n\nPRACTISE\nQ1. Convert 11001₂ to base ten. Answer: 25.\nQ2. Convert 26₁₀ to binary. Answer: 11010₂.\nQ3. Find 23₄+12₄. Answer: 101₄ (11+6=17).\nQ4. Find 20 (mod 6). Answer: 2.\nQ5. Convert 0.75₁₀ to binary. Answer: 0.11₂."}},{"id":"fractions-decimals","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Fractions, decimals and approximation","body":"Fractions: to add or subtract, use a common denominator; to multiply, multiply tops and bottoms; to divide, multiply by the reciprocal. Simplify mixed numbers into improper fractions first.\n\nDecimal places (d.p.) count digits after the point; significant figures (s.f.) count from the first non-zero digit.\n• 0.012856 to 3 s.f. = 0.0129; to 4 d.p. = 0.0129.\n• 52 461 to 2 s.f. = 52 000.\nRounding rule: look at the next digit; 5 or more rounds up.\n\nStandard form: A × 10^{n} with 1 ≤ A < 10. 0.00054 = 5.4 × 10^{-4}; 37 000 = 3.7 × 10^{4}.\n\nLong calculations without tables: write each number in standard form, group the powers of ten, and cancel before multiplying.\n\nPercentage error = (error ÷ actual value) × 100%.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Counting leading zeros as significant figures (they are not).\n• Rounding in the middle of a calculation; round only at the end.\n\nMemory tip: significant figures start at the first non-zero digit; decimal places start at the point."},"new":{"title":"Fractions, decimals and approximation","body":"INTRODUCTION\nFractions, decimals and percentages all express parts of a whole. Accuracy matters: JAMB may ask for an exact fraction, a rounded decimal or a percentage error, and each has a different rule.\n\n1. THE LANGUAGE OF FRACTIONS\nIn a/b, a is the numerator and b is the denominator (b≠0). A proper fraction is below 1 in magnitude; an improper fraction can be 1 or greater. A mixed number combines a whole number and a fraction.\nEquivalent fractions have the same value: 3/4 = 6/8. To simplify, divide numerator and denominator by their highest common factor.\n\n2. FOUR OPERATIONS\nAddition/subtraction: use the lowest common multiple of the denominators. 3/4 + 5/6 = 9/12 + 10/12 = 19/12 = 1 7/12.\nMultiplication: multiply numerators and denominators, cancelling common factors first. 7/12 × 8/21 = (7×8)/(12×21)=2/9.\nDivision: multiply by the RECIPROCAL. (3/5) ÷ (9/10) = (3/5)×(10/9)=2/3.\nConvert mixed numbers before multiplying: 2 1/3 = 7/3.\n\n3. DECIMALS, RECURRING DECIMALS AND PERCENTAGES\n0.375=375/1000=3/8=37.5%.\nTo convert 0.272727… into a fraction, let x=0.272727…; then 100x=27.272727…; subtract x to get 99x=27, so x=3/11.\nA percentage change is (new−old)/old ×100%. For an increase from ₦800 to ₦920, the increase is ₦120, or 15% of the ORIGINAL price. A 20% decrease followed by a 20% increase does not return to the initial price: 0.8×1.2=0.96, a 4% net decrease.\n\n4. APPROXIMATION\nDecimal places count digits after the point. Significant figures begin at the first non-zero digit.\n0.005786 rounded to 3 significant figures = 0.00579.\n0.005786 rounded to 3 decimal places = 0.006.\n52,748 rounded to 2 significant figures = 53,000.\nRounding to the nearest tenth is different from rounding to one significant figure.\n\n5. ACCURACY AND PERCENTAGE ERROR\nAbsolute error = |measured value − true value|.\nPercentage error = (absolute error ÷ |true value|)×100%.\nIf a length is measured as 19.6 cm instead of 20.0 cm, percentage error = 0.4/20 ×100 = 2%.\nWhen a value is rounded to the nearest whole unit, the true value lies within half a unit of the rounded number (subject to the endpoint convention). A reported length of 46 cm to the nearest centimetre means 45.5 ≤ actual length < 46.5 cm.\n\n6. RATIOS AND RATES\nFor the ratio 2:3:5, the whole is 10 parts. Divide ₦80,000 in these parts: ₦16,000, ₦24,000 and ₦40,000.\nA RATE compares unlike units, for example km/hour or ₦/kg; a ratio need not.\nTo turn 72 km/h into m/s, multiply by 1000/3600 = 5/18: answer 20 m/s.\n\nJAMB-STYLE WORKED EXAMPLE\nA shop sells 18 of 60 notebooks. Fraction sold = 18/60 = 3/10, percentage sold =30%; fraction remaining =7/10.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Adding denominators: 1/2+1/3 is 5/6, not 2/5.\n• Computing change as a percentage of the new value instead of the original.\n• Counting leading zeros as significant figures.\n• Rounding an intermediate value too early.\n• Confusing absolute error with percentage error.\n\nPRACTISE\nQ1. Express 0.125 as a fraction. Answer: 1/8.\nQ2. Compute 2 1/2 ÷ 5/6. Answer: 3.\nQ3. Round 0.04786 to 2 significant figures. Answer: 0.048.\nQ4. Find 15% of ₦2,400. Answer: ₦360.\nQ5. Convert 54 km/h to m/s. Answer: 15 m/s."}},{"id":"commercial","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Percentages, profit and loss, interest and ratio","body":"Percentage of: 15% of ₦4,000 = 0.15 × 4000 = ₦600.\nPercentage change = (change ÷ original) × 100%.\n\nProfit and loss are percentages of the COST price:\n• Profit % = (SP − CP)/CP × 100%. Selling at a 25% loss means SP = 75% of CP.\n• To find CP from SP: CP = SP × 100/(100 ± %). E.g. SP ₦180,000 at a 25% loss → CP = 180000 × 100/75 = ₦240,000.\n\nSimple interest: I = PRT/100. Amount = P + I.\nCompound interest: A = P(1 + r/100)^{n}. Depreciation: A = P(1 − r/100)^{n}.\n\nRatio and sharing: to share ₦600 in the ratio 2 : 3, total parts = 5, so shares are 240 and 360.\nProportion: if 5 men take 45 days, then (inverse) 25 men take 45 × 5/25 = 9 days.\n\nShares and dividends, commission and discount use the same idea: a percentage of the right base amount.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Taking profit or loss as a percentage of the selling price.\n• Adding percentages that apply one after another; a 10% rise then a 10% fall is a 1% fall overall.\n\nMemory tip: always ask \"percentage OF WHAT?\" — the original amount is the base."},"new":{"title":"Percentages, profit and loss, interest and ratio","body":"INTRODUCTION — FINANCIAL ARITHMETIC IN NIGERIA\nCommercial arithmetic applies percentages, ratios and rates to purchases, sales, salaries, savings, loans, taxes and investments. Always identify which amount the percentage is based on.\n\n1. COST PRICE, SELLING PRICE, PROFIT AND LOSS\nProfit = selling price − cost price. Loss = cost price − selling price.\nProfit % = (profit ÷ cost price)×100%; loss % = (loss ÷ cost price)×100%.\nA trader in Uyo buys bags for ₦80,000 and sells them for ₦100,000. Profit = ₦20,000, so profit percentage =25% of cost; profit margin =20% of selling price. Do not confuse mark-up with margin.\n\n2. DISCOUNTS AND SUCCESSIVE CHANGES\nA trade discount reduces the list price. Net price = marked price × (1−discount fraction).\nIf an item costs ₦12,000 and is discounted by 15%, the amount paid is ₦12,000×0.85=₦10,200.\nSuccessive 10% and 20% discounts give 0.9×0.8=0.72, which is 28% total discount, NOT 30%.\n\n3. VALUE ADDED TAX AND INCLUSIVE PRICES\nWhere a question specifies a VAT rate r, calculate from the stated taxable amount. VAT = net price×r; gross price = net price×(1+r). If VAT is already included, divide the gross price by (1+r) to recover the net price.\nUse the RATE PROVIDED IN THE QUESTION. Do not insert a current statutory VAT rate into an older examination problem.\n\n4. SIMPLE INTEREST\nSimple interest I=PRT/100 when R is an annual percentage and T is measured in YEARS. Amount A=P+I.\n₦50,000 saved at 8% simple interest for 9 months earns I=50,000×8×(9/12)/100=₦3,000. Amount=₦53,000.\nAn interest rate of 6% for 2 years is not the same as 6% each month; units matter.\n\n5. COMPOUND INTEREST AND DEPRECIATION\nCompound amount A=P(1+r/100)ⁿ for annual compounding, interest = A−P.\n₦20,000 at 10% compound interest for two years becomes 20,000×1.1²=₦24,200; interest=₦4,200.\nA machine loses 15% value yearly: value after n years V=P(0.85)ⁿ. This is reducing-balance depreciation, unlike equal annual simple deductions.\n\n6. SHARES, COMMISSION AND EXCHANGE RATE\nDividend = number of shares×dividend per share. For percentage dividend, check whether the percentage is based on nominal/par value or another stated base.\nA salesperson paid 3% commission on ₦1,200,000 sales earns ₦36,000.\nIf ₦1,500 exchanges for one unit of a quoted currency, ₦30,000 buys 20 units before fees. Preserve the direction of conversion.\n\n7. DIRECT AND INVERSE PROPORTION IN BUSINESS\nIf 8 identical workers make 240 units in a day, 12 workers at the same rate make 360 units. If 4 workers need 15 days, 6 equally productive workers need 10 days for the same job (worker-days unchanged).\n\nVISUAL CHECK — NET PRICE AND VAT\nThe chart below distinguishes the pre-tax amount, specified tax, and total payable.\n\nJAMB-STYLE WORKED EXAMPLE\nAn item is bought for ₦15,000, marked up by 20%, then discounted by 10%. Marked price = ₦18,000; final selling price = ₦16,200; actual profit = ₦1,200; profit percentage = 8%.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Using the selling price to calculate profit ON COST.\n• Adding consecutive discounts instead of multiplying remaining percentages.\n• Forgetting months must be converted into years in simple interest.\n• Confusing dividend rate with share price growth.\n• Failing to distinguish tax-exclusive and tax-inclusive prices.\n\nPRACTISE\nQ1. A ₦4,000 item increases by 15%. Answer: ₦4,600.\nQ2. Calculate simple interest on ₦30,000 at 5% for two years. Answer: ₦3,000.\nQ3. Find compound amount on ₦10,000 at 10% for two years. Answer: ₦12,100.\nQ4. Profit ₦6,000 on cost ₦24,000: profit percentage? Answer: 25%.\nQ5. Two successive discounts of 20% and 10% combine to what? Answer: 28%."}},{"id":"indices","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Laws of indices","body":"The laws (same base a):\n• a^{m} × a^{n} = a^{m+n}\n• a^{m} ÷ a^{n} = a^{m−n}\n• (a^{m})^{n} = a^{mn}\n• a^{0} = 1\n• a^{−n} = 1/a^{n}\n• a^{1/n} = ⁿ√a, and a^{m/n} = (ⁿ√a)^{m}\n\nEvaluating: take the root first, then the power. 27^{2/3} = (∛27)^{2} = 3^{2} = 9. 16^{−3/4} = 1/(⁴√16)^{3} = 1/8.\n\nSolving index equations: make the bases the same, then equate the powers.\n• 2^{x+1} = 32 = 2^{5} → x + 1 = 5 → x = 4.\n• 9^{x} = 27 → 3^{2x} = 3^{3} → x = 3/2.\nIf two different bases appear, write both as powers of a common prime (4 = 2^{2}, 8 = 2^{3}, 9 = 3^{2}, 27 = 3^{3}).\n\nCommon traps:\n• a^{m} + a^{n} is NOT a^{m+n}; only multiplication adds powers.\n• A negative power gives a reciprocal, not a negative number: 2^{−3} = 1/8.\n\nMemory tip: \"root first, then power\" for fractional indices; \"same base, then equate powers\" for equations."},"new":{"title":"Laws of indices","body":"INTRODUCTION\nAn index (power or exponent) tells how many times a base is multiplied by itself. 2⁴ = 2×2×2×2 =16. Indices make it possible to handle very large and very small numbers economically.\n\n1. LAWS OF INDICES (FOR NON-ZERO BASES WHERE REQUIRED)\nFor the SAME base a:\naᵐ×aⁿ=aᵐ⁺ⁿ.\naᵐ÷aⁿ=aᵐ⁻ⁿ (a≠0).\n(aᵐ)ⁿ=aᵐⁿ.\n(ab)ⁿ=aⁿbⁿ.\na⁰=1 (a≠0); a⁻ⁿ=1/aⁿ (a≠0).\na¹/ⁿ is an nth root of a (when the root is defined in the real numbers); aᵐ/ⁿ=(ⁿ√a)ᵐ.\nThese laws do not allow powers to be added across addition: 2²+2³=12, not 2⁵.\n\n2. FRACTIONAL AND NEGATIVE INDICES\n27²/³=(∛27)²=3²=9.\n16⁻³/⁴=1/(⁴√16)³=1/8.\n(-8)¹/³=-2 because (−2)³=−8. Even roots of negative numbers are not real, so be careful about the domain.\n\n3. SOLVING INDEX EQUATIONS\nMake the bases equal before equating powers.\n2^(x+1)=32=2⁵, so x+1=5 and x=4.\n9ˣ=27 → (3²)ˣ=3³ →2x=3 →x=3/2.\nWhen bases cannot easily be equated, logarithms can be used: 5ˣ=17 gives x=log 17/log 5, but do not use this unnecessarily in simple JAMB questions.\n\n4. STANDARD FORM\nScientific or standard form is A×10ⁿ, where 1≤|A|<10 and n is an integer.\n0.000072=7.2×10⁻⁵; 5,400,000=5.4×10⁶.\nMultiply coefficients and add powers; divide coefficients and subtract powers.\n(3×10⁴)(2×10⁻²)=6×10²=600.\n(9×10⁵)/(3×10²)=3×10³.\n\n5. ORDER OF OPERATIONS\nExponents are evaluated before multiplication and addition. A negative sign OUTSIDE a power differs from a negative base IN BRACKETS: −2² = −4, but (−2)² = +4.\n(2³)²=2⁶=64, whereas 2^(3²)=2⁹=512.\n\nJAMB-STYLE WORKED EXAMPLE\nSimplify 8^(2/3)×4^(−1/2): 8^(2/3)=(∛8)²=4; 4⁻¹/²=1/√4=1/2; product =2.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Adding indices while adding terms.\n• Taking a negative exponent to mean a negative value.\n• Ignoring parentheses in −a² versus (−a)².\n• Writing 0.45×10³ as standard form (coefficient less than 1).\n• Confusing 0⁰ with a⁰ for non-zero a.\n\nPRACTISE\nQ1. Simplify 3⁴÷3². Answer: 9.\nQ2. Evaluate 64¹/³. Answer: 4.\nQ3. Solve 4ˣ=32. Answer: x=5/2.\nQ4. Express 0.00056 in standard form. Answer: 5.6×10⁻⁴.\nQ5. Evaluate (−3)²−3². Answer: 0."}},{"id":"logarithms","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Logarithms","body":"Definition: log_{a} b = c means a^{c} = b. So log_{2} 8 = 3, log_{10} 0.01 = −2, log_{a} a = 1, log_{a} 1 = 0.\n\nLaws (same base):\n• log x + log y = log (xy)\n• log x − log y = log (x/y)\n• log x^{n} = n log x\n• Change of base: log_{a} b = log b / log a\n\nSimplifying: collect logs into a single log, then evaluate.\n• log_{3} 18 − log_{3} 2 = log_{3} 9 = 2.\n• 2 log 5 + log 4 = log 25 + log 4 = log 100 = 2 (base 10).\n\nSolving log equations: combine into one log, convert to index form, then CHECK the answer makes every original log defined (the inside of a log must be positive).\n• log_{2} x + log_{2} (x − 2) = 3 → x(x − 2) = 8 → x = 4 (reject x = −2).\n\nUsing tables/characteristics (older papers): the characteristic is the power of ten in standard form; a bar shows a negative characteristic, e.g. log 0.05 = 2̄.6990.\n\nCommon traps:\n• log (x + y) is NOT log x + log y.\n• Forgetting to reject solutions that make a log of a negative number.\n\nMemory tip: a log is just \"what power?\" — log_{a} b asks \"a to what power gives b?\""},"new":{"title":"Logarithms","body":"INTRODUCTION\nA logarithm answers “to what power must a base be raised to obtain this number?” If 2³=8, then log₂8=3. The exponential statement and logarithmic statement describe the same relationship.\n\n1. DEFINITION AND RESTRICTIONS\nlogₐb=c means aᶜ=b, where a>0, a≠1 and b>0 for real logarithms.\nlog₁₀1000=3; log₂(1/8)=−3; logₐ1=0; logₐa=1.\nNever take the real logarithm of zero or a negative number.\n\n2. LAWS OF LOGARITHMS\nFor positive x and y:\nlogₐ(xy)=logₐx+logₐy.\nlogₐ(x/y)=logₐx−logₐy.\nlogₐ(xⁿ)=nlogₐx.\nChange of base: logₐx=(log_b x)/(log_b a), including common logs (base 10).\nThese rules are based on laws of indices.\n\n3. EXPONENTIAL AND LOG EQUATIONS\nSolve log₂(x−1)=3: x−1=2³=8 → x=9; domain x>1 satisfied.\nSolve log₁₀x+log₁₀(x−9)=1: log₁₀[x(x−9)]=1, so x(x−9)=10, hence x²−9x−10=0=(x−10)(x+1). Only x=10 satisfies BOTH logarithm arguments being positive.\nSolve 3^(x+1)=20 with logarithms: x+1=log20/log3, so x≈1.727.\n\n4. LOGARITHM TABLES AND CHARACTERISTICS\nFor a common logarithm of a number greater than 1, the characteristic is the integer part of log₁₀x. For a number between zero and one, it is negative; the mantissa depends on the significant digits.\nlog₁₀500=log₁₀5+2≈0.6990+2=2.6990.\nlog₁₀0.05=log₁₀5−2≈−1.3010.\nKeep negative characteristics distinct from positive mantissas in tables.\n\n5. APPLICATIONS\nMany growth and decay models are exponential, and logarithms help solve for time. If an amount doubles each period, A=A₀2ᵗ. The time to become eight times as much is t=3 periods because 2³=8.\n\nJAMB-STYLE WORKED EXAMPLE\nSimplify log₃81−log₃9+log₃3 = 4−2+1 =3. Alternatively log₃[(81/9)×3]=log₃27=3.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Treating log(x+y) as log x+log y (FALSE).\n• Mixing different bases without changing base first.\n• Forgetting that log arguments must be positive.\n• Writing logₐ0=0 (undefined).\n• Keeping invalid roots after solving a logarithmic equation.\n\nPRACTISE\nQ1. Evaluate log₅125. Answer: 3.\nQ2. Solve log₃x=−2. Answer: x=1/9.\nQ3. Find log₂8+log₂4. Answer: 5.\nQ4. Evaluate log₁₀(0.001). Answer: −3.\nQ5. Solve log₂(x+1)=4. Answer: x=15."}},{"id":"surds","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Surds","body":"A surd is a root that cannot be simplified to a whole number, like √2.\n\nSimplify by taking out square factors: √72 = √(36 × 2) = 6√2. √48 = 4√3.\n\nAdd and subtract only like surds: 3√2 + 5√2 = 8√2. First simplify: √50 + √18 = 5√2 + 3√2 = 8√2.\n\nMultiply: √a × √b = √(ab); (√a)^{2} = a. Expand brackets as in algebra, e.g. (√3 + √5)(√3 − √5) = 3 − 5 = −2.\n\nRationalise the denominator:\n• 1/√2 = √2/2.\n• For a + √b, multiply top and bottom by its conjugate a − √b: 1/(2 + √3) = (2 − √3)/(4 − 3) = 2 − √3.\n\nCommon traps:\n• √a + √b is NOT √(a + b).\n• Forgetting that (√a + √b)^{2} = a + b + 2√(ab), not just a + b.\n\nMemory tip: to clear a surd from the bottom, multiply by its \"partner with the opposite sign\"."},"new":{"title":"Surds","body":"INTRODUCTION\nA surd is an irrational root that cannot be expressed as an exact rational number, such as √2 or √5. Simplifying surds preserves exact values; rounding too early can produce the wrong JAMB answer.\n\n1. SIMPLIFYING SURDS\nWrite the radicand as a perfect square times a remainder.\n√72 = √(36×2)=6√2.\n√200=10√2; ∛54=3∛2.\n√18+√8=3√2+2√2=5√2. Only LIKE surds can be added.\n\n2. MULTIPLYING AND DIVIDING\n√a√b=√(ab) for non-negative a,b.\n(2√3)(5√6)=10√18=30√2.\n(√7+√2)(√7−√2)=7−2=5 — an application of (a+b)(a−b)=a²−b².\nIn general √a+√b ≠ √(a+b).\n\n3. RATIONALISING THE DENOMINATOR\nA denominator should usually be expressed without a surd:\n3/√5=(3√5)/5.\nFor a binomial denominator multiply by the CONJUGATE:\n2/(√3+1)=2(√3−1)/(3−1)=√3−1.\nSimilarly 1/(√5−√2)=(√5+√2)/(5−2)=(√5+√2)/3.\n\n4. EQUATIONS INVOLVING SURDS\nIf √(x+4)=x−2, the right-hand side requires x≥2. Square both sides:\nx+4=x²−4x+4 → x²−5x=0 → x=0 or x=5.\nOnly x=5 is valid in the ORIGINAL equation. Checking after squaring is essential because squaring can introduce an invalid solution.\nFor √(x+1)=3, x+1=9, so x=8.\n\n5. ESTIMATING WITHOUT LOSING PRECISION\nBecause 1.4²=1.96 and 1.5²=2.25, √2 lies between 1.4 and 1.5. But in an exact-answer question leave √2 unchanged until the final step.\n\nJAMB-STYLE WORKED EXAMPLE\nSimplify (√12+√27)/√3 = (2√3+3√3)/√3 =5.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Adding unlike surds as if they were like terms.\n• Forgetting to multiply the ENTIRE numerator by the conjugate.\n• Assuming √(a+b)=√a+√b.\n• Losing the minus sign in difference of squares.\n• Failing to check roots after squaring an equation.\n\nPRACTISE\nQ1. Simplify √48. Answer: 4√3.\nQ2. Simplify √75−√12. Answer: 3√3.\nQ3. Rationalise 1/√2. Answer: √2/2.\nQ4. Evaluate (√11+√7)(√11−√7). Answer: 4.\nQ5. Solve √(x+5)=4. Answer: x=11."}},{"id":"sets","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Sets and Venn diagrams","body":"Notation: ∪ union (in either), ∩ intersection (in both), A′ complement (not in A), ⊂ subset, ∅ empty set, n(A) number of elements.\n\nTwo sets: n(A ∪ B) = n(A) + n(B) − n(A ∩ B).\nExample: 220 students take Biology or Maths; 125 take Biology and 110 take Maths. Both = 125 + 110 − 220 = 15, so Biology only = 125 − 15 = 110.\n\nThree sets: draw a Venn diagram and fill it from the CENTRE outwards: first all three, then each pair (minus the centre), then each \"only\" region. Use x for the unknown region and form one equation from the total.\n\nNumber of subsets of a set with n elements = 2^{n} (including ∅ and the set itself); proper subsets = 2^{n} − 1.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Forgetting to subtract the overlap, so elements in both sets are counted twice.\n• Confusing \"only A\" with \"A\".\n\nMemory tip: in Venn questions, fill the middle first and work outwards."},"new":{"title":"Sets and Venn diagrams","body":"INTRODUCTION\nA set is a well-defined collection of distinct objects. Sets are useful for classifying students, subjects, clubs, goods and other items. JAMB often tests both symbols and three-set Venn diagrams, so visual organisation matters.\n\n1. SET LANGUAGE\nA={1,2,3}; 2∈A means 2 belongs to A. 5∉A means 5 is not in A.\n∅ is the empty set. U is the universal set. A⊆B means every member of A is also in B; the set A is itself a subset of A.\nn(A) is the number of distinct elements of set A. Repeated listing does not increase n(A).\nThe power set of a set with n elements has 2ⁿ subsets, INCLUDING the empty set and the original set.\n\n2. UNION, INTERSECTION, COMPLEMENT AND DIFFERENCE\nA∪B contains everything in A or B (or both).\nA∩B contains members shared by A and B.\nA′ contains members in the universal set that are NOT in A.\nA\\B contains members in A but NOT in B.\nFor A={1,2,3,4}, B={3,4,5}, U={1,2,3,4,5,6}:\nA∪B={1,2,3,4,5}; A∩B={3,4}; A\\B={1,2}; A′={5,6}.\n\n3. TWO-SET COUNTING\nn(A∪B)=n(A)+n(B)−n(A∩B).\nIn a class of 45, 24 take Economics, 20 take Government and 8 take both:\nEconomics only=24−8=16; Government only=20−8=12; at least one=24+20−8=36; neither=45−36=9.\nThe overlap must be SUBTRACTED once in the union formula, because it was counted twice.\n\n4. THREE-SET VENN DIAGRAMS\nFill the central triple intersection first. Next fill each pairwise overlap excluding the middle, then each single-set-only region. Compute the outside last.\nFor sets A,B,C:\nn(A∪B∪C)=n(A)+n(B)+n(C)−n(A∩B)−n(A∩C)−n(B∩C)+n(A∩B∩C).\nIf a question says “A and B” without saying “A and B only”, their count includes those who may also be in C.\n\n5. LAWS AND PROPERTIES\nCommutative: A∪B=B∪A, A∩B=B∩A.\nDistributive: A∩(B∪C)=(A∩B)∪(A∩C).\nDe Morgan: (A∪B)′=A′∩B′ and (A∩B)′=A′∪B′.\nDisjoint sets have no common member: A∩B=∅.\n\nVISUAL CHECK — TWO-SET VENN REGIONS\nThe diagram below uses the 45-student example. Every number belongs in exactly ONE region: Economics only 16, both 8, Government only 12, neither 9.\n\nJAMB-STYLE WORKED EXAMPLE\nIf n(A)=30, n(B)=25 and n(A∪B)=43, then n(A∩B)=30+25−43=12.\n\nCOMMON TRAPS\n• Confusing union (OR) with intersection (AND).\n• Putting the total for A directly in A-only.\n• Forgetting to include neither when the question gives the universal total.\n• Treating a repeated item in a set as two different elements.\n• Forgetting to add the triple intersection back in the three-set formula.\n\nPRACTISE\nQ1. If A={2,4,6} and B={4,6,8}, find A∩B. Answer: {4,6}.\nQ2. If n(A)=19, n(B)=18, n(A∩B)=7, find n(A∪B). Answer: 30.\nQ3. A set has 4 distinct elements. How many subsets? Answer: 16.\nQ4. In a class of 50, 40 study either or both of two subjects. How many study neither? Answer: 10.\nQ5. What is A∩A′? Answer: ∅."}},{"id":"algebra","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Algebraic expressions and functions","body":"Expanding: (a + b)^{2} = a^{2} + 2ab + b^{2}; (a − b)^{2} = a^{2} − 2ab + b^{2}; (a + b)(a − b) = a^{2} − b^{2}.\n\nFactorising: take out common factors first; spot the difference of two squares; for ax^{2} + bx + c, find two numbers with product ac and sum b.\n\nAlgebraic fractions: factorise top and bottom, then cancel common FACTORS (never terms). Add with a common denominator.\n\nChange of subject: undo the operations around the required letter in reverse order, doing the same to both sides.\n• From T = 2π√(l/g): T/(2π) = √(l/g) → T^{2}/(4π^{2}) = l/g → l = gT^{2}/(4π^{2}).\n\nFunctions: f(x) = 2x + 3 means f(4) = 11. Composite f(g(x)): put g(x) into f. Inverse f^{−1}: swap x and y and solve for y.\n\nRemainder and factor theorems: the remainder when f(x) is divided by (x − a) is f(a); (x − a) is a factor if f(a) = 0.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Cancelling terms instead of factors: (x + 2)/2 is not x + 1.\n• Sign errors when expanding a minus outside a bracket.\n\nMemory tip: factorise before you cancel, and substitute to check."},"new":{"title":"Algebraic expressions and functions","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"equations","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Linear and simultaneous equations","body":"Linear: collect x-terms on one side and numbers on the other. With fractions, multiply every term by the LCM of the denominators first.\n\nSimultaneous equations:\n• Elimination: make the coefficients of one letter equal, then add or subtract the equations.\n• Substitution: make one letter the subject of one equation and substitute into the other.\nExample: 2x + y = 7 and x − y = 2 → adding gives 3x = 9, x = 3, y = 1.\n\nWord problems: let x be the unknown, write the equation from the words, solve, and answer the question asked.\n\nChecking: substitute your answers into BOTH original equations. In a JAMB question you can also test each option.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Multiplying only some terms by the LCM.\n• Answering x when the question asks for y, or for x + y.\n\nMemory tip: with four options, substituting each option back is often the fastest method."},"new":{"title":"Linear and simultaneous equations","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"quadratics","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Quadratic equations","body":"Standard form: ax^{2} + bx + c = 0.\n\nSolving:\n• Factorisation: x^{2} − 5x + 6 = 0 → (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0 → x = 2 or 3.\n• Formula: x = [−b ± √(b^{2} − 4ac)]/(2a).\n• Completing the square.\n\nRoots α and β: α + β = −b/a, αβ = c/a. The equation with roots α and β is x^{2} − (α + β)x + αβ = 0.\nExample: roots 2 and −3 → x^{2} + x − 6 = 0.\nUseful: α^{2} + β^{2} = (α + β)^{2} − 2αβ.\n\nThe discriminant b^{2} − 4ac: positive → two real roots; zero → equal roots; negative → no real roots.\n\nCubics in JAMB: find one root by testing small numbers (f(1), f(−1), …), then factorise. For x^{3} − 5x^{2} − x + 5 = 0, f(1) = 0, f(−1) = 0, f(5) = 0, so x = 1, −1 or 5.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sign of the sum: α + β = −b/a (note the minus).\n• Dividing both sides by x and losing the root x = 0.\n\nMemory tip: \"sum = −b/a, product = c/a\"."},"new":{"title":"Quadratic equations","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"variation","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Variation","body":"• Direct: y ∝ x → y = kx. If x doubles, y doubles.\n• Inverse: y ∝ 1/x → y = k/x. If x doubles, y halves.\n• Joint: y ∝ xz → y = kxz.\n• Partial: y is partly constant and partly varies with x → y = a + bx (two unknowns, so you need two pairs of values).\nPowers and roots: \"y varies as the square of x\" → y = kx^{2}; \"inversely as the square root of x\" → y = k/√x.\n\nMethod (always the same):\n1. Write the equation with k.\n2. Use the given pair of values to find k.\n3. Use k to find the unknown.\n\nExample: y varies inversely as x^{2}; y = 4 when x = 3. Then 4 = k/9, k = 36. When x = 6, y = 36/36 = 1.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Mixing up direct and inverse.\n• For partial variation, forgetting the constant term.\n\nMemory tip: \"find k first\" — every variation question is solved by finding the constant."},"new":{"title":"Variation","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"inequalities","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Inequalities and linear programming","body":"Solve like equations, EXCEPT: multiplying or dividing by a negative number reverses the sign.\n• −3x > 6 → x < −2.\n\nDouble inequalities: do the same to all three parts. −1 < 2x + 3 ≤ 7 → −4 < 2x ≤ 4 → −2 < x ≤ 2.\n\nQuadratic inequalities: find the roots, then test a value in each region. (x − 1)(x − 4) < 0 → 1 < x < 4.\n\nOn a number line or graph: an open circle or dotted line = strict (< or >); a closed circle or solid line = ≤ or ≥.\n\nLinear programming: graph each inequality, find the region satisfying all of them, then test the corners of that region in the objective (e.g. maximise P = 3x + 2y). The maximum or minimum is always at a corner.\n\nTo find an inequality from a shaded graph: get the line's equation from two points, then test a point inside the region (like (0, 0)) to choose the sign.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Forgetting to flip the sign when dividing by a negative.\n• Testing a point that lies on the boundary line.\n\nMemory tip: negative multiplier → flip the sign; in linear programming, the answer lives at a corner."},"new":{"title":"Inequalities and linear programming","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"sequences","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Sequences: AP and GP","body":"Arithmetic progression (AP): constant difference d.\n• nth term: T_{n} = a + (n − 1)d\n• Sum: S_{n} = n/2 [2a + (n − 1)d] = n/2 (first + last)\n\nGeometric progression (GP): constant ratio r.\n• nth term: T_{n} = ar^{n−1}\n• Sum: S_{n} = a(r^{n} − 1)/(r − 1)\n• Sum to infinity (only when −1 < r < 1): S_{∞} = a/(1 − r)\n\nFrom a sum formula: T_{n} = S_{n} − S_{n−1}.\nMeans: the arithmetic mean of a and b is (a + b)/2; the geometric mean is √(ab).\n\nFinding a and d (or r): turn the two facts into two equations. E.g. T_{3} = 7 and T_{7} = 19 → 4d = 12, d = 3, a = 1.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Using n instead of (n − 1) in the nth-term formula.\n• Using the sum to infinity when |r| ≥ 1.\n\nMemory tip: AP = adding the same number; GP = multiplying by the same number."},"new":{"title":"Sequences: AP and GP","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"binary-operations","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Binary operations","body":"A binary operation * combines two elements using a given rule, e.g. a * b = a + b + 2ab.\n\nEvaluating: substitute carefully. With that rule, 2 * 3 = 2 + 3 + 12 = 17. Work out brackets first: (1 * 2) * 3.\n\nProperties:\n• Closure: the result is always in the set.\n• Commutative: a * b = b * a for all a, b.\n• Associative: (a * b) * c = a * (b * c).\n• Identity e: a * e = a for every a. Solve a * e = a for e.\n• Inverse of a: the element a^{−1} with a * a^{−1} = e.\n\nExample: for a * b = a + b + 2ab, a * e = a gives e + 2ae = 0, so e = 0. The inverse of a satisfies a + x + 2ax = 0, so x = −a/(1 + 2a).\n\nTables: the identity's row repeats the header row; check symmetry about the main diagonal for commutativity.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Treating * as ordinary multiplication.\n• Finding the inverse before finding the identity.\n\nMemory tip: identity first (a * e = a), then inverse (a * x = e)."},"new":{"title":"Binary operations","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"matrices","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Matrices and determinants","body":"Adding and subtracting: matrices must be the same size; combine matching entries.\n\nMultiplying: row × column. A (2 × 3) times a (3 × 2) gives a 2 × 2. The number of columns of the first must equal the number of rows of the second. In general AB ≠ BA.\n\n2 × 2 matrix with rows (a, b) and (c, d):\n• Determinant = ad − bc.\n• Inverse = 1/(ad − bc) × [rows (d, −b) and (−c, a)]. Swap a and d, negate b and c, divide by the determinant.\n• A matrix with determinant 0 is singular and has no inverse.\n\n3 × 3 determinant: expand along the top row with alternating signs + − +, using the 2 × 2 determinants of the minors.\n\nSolving simultaneous equations with matrices: X = A^{−1}B, or use Cramer's rule.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Sign errors in the alternating + − + pattern.\n• Assuming AB = BA.\n\nMemory tip: inverse of a 2 × 2 is \"swap the diagonal, negate the others, divide by the determinant\"."},"new":{"title":"Matrices and determinants","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"geometry","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Angles, polygons, triangles and circles","body":"Angles: on a straight line add to 180°; at a point add to 360°; vertically opposite angles are equal. With parallel lines: corresponding angles are equal, alternate angles are equal, co-interior angles add to 180°.\n\nTriangles: angles add to 180°; an exterior angle equals the sum of the two interior opposite angles. Isosceles: base angles are equal. Similar triangles: corresponding sides are in the same ratio.\n\nPolygons (n sides): interior angles add to (n − 2) × 180°; exterior angles add to 360°. Each exterior angle of a regular polygon = 360°/n.\n\nCircle theorems:\n• The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference on the same arc.\n• Angles in the same segment are equal.\n• The angle in a semicircle is 90°.\n• Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to 180°.\n• A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact; two tangents from a point are equal.\n• Alternate segment theorem: the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment.\n\nLoci: points a fixed distance from a point form a circle; equidistant from two points form the perpendicular bisector; equidistant from two lines form the angle bisector.\n\nMemory tip: in circle questions, mark the centre and radii first; most answers come from isosceles triangles and \"centre = 2 × circumference\"."},"new":{"title":"Angles, polygons, triangles and circles","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"mensuration","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Lengths, areas and volumes","body":"Plane shapes:\n• Rectangle A = lb; triangle A = ½bh = ½ab sin C; parallelogram A = bh; trapezium A = ½(a + b)h.\n• Circle: C = 2πr, A = πr^{2}.\n• Arc length = (θ/360) × 2πr; sector area = (θ/360) × πr^{2}.\n\nSolids:\n• Cuboid V = lbh; cylinder V = πr^{2}h, curved surface = 2πrh.\n• Cone V = ⅓πr^{2}h, curved surface = πrl (l = slant height, l^{2} = r^{2} + h^{2}).\n• Sphere V = 4/3 πr^{3}, surface = 4πr^{2}. Pyramid V = ⅓ × base area × height.\n• Frustum = big cone − small cone.\n\nA sector folded into a cone: the arc length becomes the base circumference, and the sector radius becomes the slant height.\n\nSimilar shapes: if lengths scale by k, areas scale by k^{2} and volumes by k^{3}.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Using the diameter instead of the radius.\n• Mixing units (cm and m) in one calculation.\n\nMemory tip: check units: length (cm), area (cm²), volume (cm³). The answer's unit tells you which formula family you need."},"new":{"title":"Lengths, areas and volumes","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"loci","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Loci and geometric constructions","body":"This syllabus objective did not previously have a dedicated Mathematics lesson."},"new":{"title":"Loci and geometric constructions","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"coordinate","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Coordinate geometry of straight lines","body":"For points (x_{1}, y_{1}) and (x_{2}, y_{2}):\n• Gradient m = (y_{2} − y_{1})/(x_{2} − x_{1}).\n• Distance = √[(x_{2} − x_{1})^{2} + (y_{2} − y_{1})^{2}].\n• Midpoint = ((x_{1} + x_{2})/2, (y_{1} + y_{2})/2).\n\nEquation of a line: y = mx + c (c = y-intercept), or y − y_{1} = m(x − x_{1}).\nFrom ax + by + c = 0, the gradient is −a/b.\n\nParallel lines have equal gradients. Perpendicular lines: m_{1} × m_{2} = −1.\n\nGradient of a curve at a point = gradient of the tangent there (use differentiation).\n\nIntercepts: put x = 0 to find the y-intercept; put y = 0 to find the x-intercept.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Subtracting coordinates in different orders on the top and bottom of the gradient.\n• Forgetting the minus sign in −a/b.\n\nMemory tip: gradient = \"rise over run\"; perpendicular = \"flip and change the sign\"."},"new":{"title":"Coordinate geometry of straight lines","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"trigonometry","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Trigonometry","body":"Right-angled triangles: SOH CAH TOA. sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent.\n\nSpecial angles: sin 30° = ½, cos 30° = √3/2, tan 30° = 1/√3; sin 45° = cos 45° = 1/√2, tan 45° = 1; sin 60° = √3/2, cos 60° = ½, tan 60° = √3.\n\nSigns by quadrant (All, Sin, Tan, Cos for quadrants 1–4): only sin is positive between 90° and 180°, so sin 150° = sin 30° = ½ but cos 150° = −√3/2.\n\nIdentities: sin^{2} θ + cos^{2} θ = 1; tan θ = sin θ/cos θ. If sin θ = 3/5 (acute), draw a 3-4-5 triangle: cos θ = 4/5, tan θ = 3/4.\n\nAny triangle: sine rule a/sin A = b/sin B; cosine rule a^{2} = b^{2} + c^{2} − 2bc cos A; area = ½ab sin C.\n\nBearings are measured clockwise from north, written with three figures (e.g. 060°). Angles of elevation and depression are measured from the horizontal.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Calculator in the wrong mode, or forgetting the quadrant sign.\n• Measuring a bearing from the wrong point.\n\nMemory tip: draw the triangle; for an acute angle given as a fraction, use a Pythagorean triple (3-4-5, 5-12-13)."},"new":{"title":"Trigonometry","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"differentiation","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Differentiation","body":"Power rule: d/dx (x^{n}) = nx^{n−1}. Constants differentiate to 0.\n• y = 3x^{2} − 2x + 5 → dy/dx = 6x − 2.\n\nStandard results: d/dx (sin x) = cos x; d/dx (cos x) = −sin x; d/dx (e^{x}) = e^{x}; d/dx (ln x) = 1/x.\n\nRules:\n• Chain rule: d/dx f(g(x)) = f′(g(x)) × g′(x). E.g. y = sin^{2} 5x → dy/dx = 2 sin 5x × cos 5x × 5 = 10 sin 5x cos 5x.\n• Product rule: (uv)′ = u′v + uv′. Quotient rule: (u/v)′ = (u′v − uv′)/v^{2}.\n\nUses:\n• The gradient of the tangent at a point = dy/dx at that x.\n• Turning points: dy/dx = 0; the second derivative tells max (negative) or min (positive).\n• Rates of change: dA/dt = dA/dr × dr/dt. E.g. A = πr^{2}, r = 5, dr/dt = 0.2 → dA/dt = 2π(5)(0.2) = 2π.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Forgetting the \"inner derivative\" in the chain rule.\n• Writing d/dx(cos x) = sin x (it is −sin x).\n\nMemory tip: \"power down in front, reduce the power by one\", then multiply by the inside's derivative."},"new":{"title":"Differentiation","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"applications-differentiation","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Applications of differentiation: rates, maxima and minima","body":"This syllabus objective did not previously have a dedicated Mathematics lesson."},"new":{"title":"Applications of differentiation: rates, maxima and minima","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"integration","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Integration","body":"Integration reverses differentiation.\n\nPower rule: ∫ x^{n} dx = x^{n+1}/(n + 1) + C (n ≠ −1). ∫ 1/x dx = ln x + C.\nStandard results: ∫ sin x dx = −cos x + C; ∫ cos x dx = sin x + C; ∫ e^{x} dx = e^{x} + C.\nWith ax inside: ∫ sin 3x dx = −⅓ cos 3x + C (divide by the coefficient of x).\n\nDefinite integrals: ∫_{a}^{b} f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a). No + C is needed.\n• ∫_{0}^{2} 3x^{2} dx = [x^{3}] from 0 to 2 = 8.\n\nArea under a curve between x = a and x = b = ∫_{a}^{b} y dx. A region below the x-axis gives a negative value; take its size.\n\nFinding y from dy/dx: integrate, then use the given point to find C.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Dividing by n instead of n + 1.\n• Forgetting the minus sign in ∫ sin x dx = −cos x.\n\nMemory tip: to check an integral, differentiate your answer; you should get back the original."},"new":{"title":"Integration","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"representation-data","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Representation of data: tables, histograms, bar and pie charts","body":"This syllabus objective did not previously have a dedicated Mathematics lesson."},"new":{"title":"Representation of data: tables, histograms, bar and pie charts","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"measures-location","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Measures of location: averages and ogives","body":"This syllabus objective did not previously have a dedicated Mathematics lesson."},"new":{"title":"Measures of location: averages and ogives","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"measures-dispersion","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Measures of dispersion: range, variance and standard deviation","body":"This syllabus objective did not previously have a dedicated Mathematics lesson."},"new":{"title":"Measures of dispersion: range, variance and standard deviation","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"statistics","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Statistics","body":"Measures of central tendency:\n• Mean = Σx/n; for a frequency table, mean = Σfx/Σf.\n• Median: the middle value when the data are arranged in order (average the two middle values for an even count).\n• Mode: the most frequent value.\n\nSpread:\n• Range = highest − lowest.\n• Variance = Σ(x − x̄)^{2}/n; standard deviation = √variance.\n• Mean deviation = Σ|x − x̄|/n.\n\nGrouped data: use class mid-points as x. Cumulative frequency curves (ogives) give the median (at n/2) and quartiles (at n/4 and 3n/4); interquartile range = Q_{3} − Q_{1}.\n\nCharts: in a pie chart, a sector angle = (frequency/total) × 360°. Histograms use areas; for equal class widths, bar heights show frequencies.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Forgetting to sort the data before finding the median.\n• Using Σx/n instead of Σfx/Σf for a frequency table.\n\nMemory tip: the mean uses every value; the median uses position; the mode uses frequency."},"new":{"title":"Statistics","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"permutations","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Permutations and combinations","body":"Factorial: n! = n × (n − 1) × … × 1; 0! = 1.\n\nPermutations (order matters): ⁿP_{r} = n!/(n − r)!. The ways to arrange 3 of 5 books on a shelf = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60.\nCombinations (order does not matter): ⁿC_{r} = n!/[r!(n − r)!]. The ways to choose 3 of 5 students = 10.\n\nArranging letters with repeats: divide by the factorial of each repeat. In \"ADDED\", 5!/(3!) = 20 (three Ds).\n\nCommittees with conditions: multiply the choices from each group. 2 men from 5 and 1 woman from 4 = ⁵C_{2} × ⁴C_{1} = 10 × 4 = 40.\n\nCircular arrangements of n objects: (n − 1)!.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Using permutations when the order does not matter (committees and selections are combinations).\n• Forgetting to divide for repeated letters.\n\nMemory tip: \"arrange → permutation; choose → combination\"."},"new":{"title":"Permutations and combinations","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}},{"id":"probability","revised":false,"old":{"title":"Probability","body":"P(event) = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes. 0 ≤ P ≤ 1, and P(not A) = 1 − P(A).\n\nAND (independent events): multiply. P(two heads) = ½ × ½ = ¼.\nOR (mutually exclusive events): add. P(a 2 or a 5 on a die) = 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3.\nIn general, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).\n\nWithout replacement, the second probability changes: from 5 red and 3 blue, P(two reds) = 5/8 × 4/7 = 5/14.\n\nTwo dice: there are 36 outcomes. List them in a 6 × 6 table for sums and differences (a sum of 7 occurs 6 times, so P = 1/6).\n\n\"At least one\": P(at least one) = 1 − P(none).\n\nCommon traps:\n• Adding when you should multiply (AND = multiply).\n• Forgetting to reduce the totals when items are not replaced.\n\nMemory tip: AND → ×, OR → +, \"at least one\" → 1 − none."},"new":{"title":"Probability","body":"This lesson is scheduled for the next Mathematics revision batch. The current original lesson is available on the left under Compare, and its existing mapped questions have been retained. It will receive complete definitions, methods, worked examples, JAMB traps, and diagrams where required before being marked revised."}}];
window.MATHEMATICS_REVIEW_DIAGRAMS={"number-bases":"<div class=\"math-diagram\"><h3>Binary place values: 1011₂</h3><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 152\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Binary columns: 1 times 8, 0 times 4, 1 times 2, 1 times 1 equals 11\"><g font-family=\"system-ui\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#18324c\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"640\" height=\"130\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f4ff\"/><text x=\"95\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"18\">2³ = 8</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"18\">2² = 4</text><text x=\"390\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"18\">2¹ = 2</text><text x=\"535\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"18\">2⁰ = 1</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"28\">1</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"28\">0</text><text x=\"390\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"28\">1</text><text x=\"535\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"28\">1</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"700\">8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11₁₀</text></g></svg></div>","commercial":"<div class=\"math-diagram\"><h3>Sale price: net amount + stated tax</h3><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 140\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Net price 10000 naira plus example five percent tax 500 naira equals 10500 naira\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"15\" width=\"500\" height=\"66\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#e7f4ed\"/><rect x=\"510\" y=\"15\" width=\"140\" height=\"66\" rx=\"11\" fill=\"#f8e7cd\"/><g font-family=\"system-ui\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#12324d\"><text x=\"255\" y=\"42\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"700\">Net price · ₦10,000</text><text x=\"580\" y=\"42\" font-size=\"15\">Tax 5%</text><text x=\"580\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"17\">₦500</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"700\">Total = ₦10,500 (example rate only)</text></g></svg></div>","sets":"<div class=\"math-diagram\"><h3>45 students: Economics and Government</h3><svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Rectangle universal set 45. Circle Economics has 16 only, overlap 8, Government 12 only, outside neither 9\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"640\" height=\"270\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fafcff\" stroke=\"#324f6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"268\" cy=\"145\" r=\"105\" fill=\"#cae5ff\" fill-opacity=\".8\" stroke=\"#316a99\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"391\" cy=\"145\" r=\"105\" fill=\"#fce8c4\" fill-opacity=\".8\" stroke=\"#b97c17\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><g font-family=\"system-ui\" fill=\"#142b45\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"234\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"16\">Economics</text><text x=\"430\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"16\">Government</text><text x=\"206\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"29\">16</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"29\" font-weight=\"700\">8</text><text x=\"453\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"29\">12</text><text x=\"66\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"18\">U = 45</text><text x=\"576\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"18\">9 neither</text></g></svg></div>"};
